---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const modelCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: 'P0*exp(k*x)', label: 'unlimited growth', color: '#b42318' },
    { expr: 'pmax/(1 + 9*exp(-k*x))', label: 'logistic (resource-limited)', color: '#1f6f54', width: 2.2 },
  ],
  params: [
    { name: 'P0', label: 'initial P₀', min: 0.1, max: 2, step: 0.1, value: 0.5 },
    { name: 'k', label: 'growth rate k', min: 0.1, max: 1, step: 0.05, value: 0.4 },
    { name: 'pmax', label: 'carrying capacity', min: 5, max: 20, step: 1, value: 10 },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 25, label: 'time' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 22 },
  crossings: false,
  height: 350,
};

const sepCfg = {
  title: 'Worked example: solving dP/dt = kP by separation',
  steps: [
    { math: 'dP/dt = kP', note: 'the law: growth rate proportional to current size.' },
    { math: '(1/P) dP = k dt', note: 'separation: herd all P to the left, all t to the right.' },
    { math: '∫ (1/P) dP = ∫ k dt  →  ln P = kt + C', note: 'integrate both sides independently — this is where the previous lesson pays off.' },
    { math: 'P = e^(kt + C) = e^C · e^(kt) = A·e^(kt)', note: 'exponentiate. The constant hides in A: every solution is an exponential.' },
    { math: 'P(0) = P₀  →  A = P₀', note: 'initial conditions select one member of the family: P(t) = P₀·e^(kt).' },
    { math: 'check: dP/dt = k·P₀·e^(kt) = kP ✓', note: 'always verify by substituting back into the original equation.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/math/advanced/differential-equations">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an advanced Mathematics lesson. It assumes the intermediate lesson <em>The
    Derivative</em> — slope as an instantaneous rate of change — and the intermediate
    <em>Exponentials &amp; Logarithms</em> lesson, whose curves are about to become the answers
    to equations rather than graphs to sketch. The foundational lessons on linear functions and
    quadratics supply the habit of reading graphs as statements about rates.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>read a differential equation as a rule for the slope at every point, and name its order;</li>
    <li>solve separable first-order equations and recover the exponential, growth, decay and cooling laws from it;</li>
    <li>solve linear equations that refuse to separate, with the integrating factor;</li>
    <li>interpret second-order solutions — damping regimes and resonance — physically;</li>
    <li>classify equilibria as stable or unstable from a phase line, and run Euler's method by hand.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Nature writes its laws as <strong>differential equations</strong>: statements about how
    things change, not where things are. Newton's second law is the archetype — force
    determines <em>acceleration</em> (the second derivative of position), so predicting
    where a thrown ball lands means finding a function whose second derivative matches the
    forces. The same pattern runs through every science: "the population grows at a rate
    proportional to its size" is dP/dt = kP; "the reaction speeds up in proportion to the
    reactant still present" is the first-order kinetics law; "the capacitor charges at a
    rate set by what is still missing" is the RC circuit equation. A differential equation
    is a rule for the slope at every point of a graph; <strong>solving</strong> it means
    finding the graph whose slope obeys the rule everywhere.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two vocabularies before the techniques. The <strong>order</strong> of an equation is the
    highest derivative appearing (dP/dt = kP is first-order; F = ma is second-order). A
    <strong>general solution</strong> is a family of functions containing undetermined
    constants; an <strong>initial condition</strong> ("P(0) = 500") pins the constants down
    to the one <strong>particular solution</strong> that matches reality. This mirrors the
    +C of integration: a law of change describes a family of futures, and the present state
    chooses among them. Determinism, as an idea, is exactly the statement that an equation
    plus initial conditions has a unique solution.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="two answers to the same kind of law — unchecked growth versus growth capped by a carrying capacity" widget="plotter" config={modelCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> three sliders, two curves, one contrast. Raising k makes
    both curves climb sooner — the growth rate is the lever — but only the green logistic curve
    knows about the ceiling: drag the carrying capacity and the red exponential ignores it
    completely, while the green curve flattens at exactly pₘₐₓ whatever you do to k. That
    insensitivity of the ceiling to the growth rate is the whole difference between the two laws.
  </p>

  <p>
    The red curve solves dP/dt = kP exactly. The green <strong>logistic</strong> curve
    solves the corrected law dP/dt = kP(1 − P/pₘₐₓ), where growth slows as the population
    nears its ceiling: the factor (1 − P/pₘₐₓ) is the fraction of the resource still free.
    Bacteria in a flask, adopters of a technology, learners building vocabulary, epidemics
    in their early weeks — wherever a growth rate is proportional both to what exists and
    to what remains, the S-curve appears. Drag the sliders: raising k compresses time but
    changes nothing structural, and the curve always levels off at exactly pₘₐₓ.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> a differential equation is a slope rule, and the same kind of law
    with one corrective factor produces either runaway growth or an S-curve — the model decides
    which world you live in. Next, the first technique for solving such laws exactly.
  </p>

  <h2>First-order equations I: separation of variables</h2>
  <p>
    The workhorse for first-order equations that can be written as
    (stuff in y)·dy/dx = (stuff in x): push every y to one side, every x to the other, then
    integrate both sides. It looks illegal — treating dy/dx as a fraction — but it is a
    shorthand for a legitimate substitution, and it works. Watch it produce the exponential
    from nothing:
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="separation in six steps — from a law of change to P(t) = P₀·e^(kt)" widget="equationStep" config={sepCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> watch where the constant lives. After integrating, the +C
    sits in the exponent; exponentiating turns it into a multiplier A; and the initial condition
    in step 5 finally fixes A to the starting value P₀. Step 6 is the habit to steal: substitute
    the answer back into the original equation and check the slope rule holds — verification is
    cheap and catches every algebra slip.
  </p>

  <p>
    The same recipe solves the <strong>half-life and doubling-time</strong> questions. With
    P = P₀·e^(kt), the time to double satisfies 2 = e^(kT), so T = ln 2 / k ≈ 0.693/k — the
    <strong>rule of 70</strong> from economics (70 divided by the percentage growth rate)
    is this formula in disguise. Worked numbers: money growing at 5% per year with annual
    compounding-of-continuously, k = 0.05, doubles in ln 2/0.05 ≈ 13.9 years. Radioactive
    decay obeys the same equation with k negative: if k = −0.01 per year, the half-life is
    0.693/0.010 ≈ 69 years, and after four half-lives (about 277 years) 1/16 of the sample
    remains. One equation, both stories — growth and decay are the same mathematics with
    opposite signs.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> separation turned one slope rule into the exponential family, and
    the same family told both the doubling-time and the half-life story. Next, the equations
    separation cannot touch — and the trick that solves them anyway.
  </p>
  <p>
    Newton's <strong>law of cooling</strong> is the next classic: dT/dt = −k(T − T_room),
    the rate of heat loss proportional to the temperature <em>difference</em>. Separating
    (let u = T − T_room, then du/dt = −ku) gives T(t) = T_room + (T₀ − T_room)·e^(−kt): the
    excess temperature decays exponentially. Worked example: coffee at 90 °C in a 20 °C room
    with k = 0.07 per minute. Excess = 70 °C initially; after 10 minutes the excess is
    70·e^(−0.7) ≈ 34.7, so T ≈ 54.7 °C; after 30 minutes the excess is
    70·e^(−2.1) ≈ 8.6, so T ≈ 28.6 °C. Cooling is fast while hot and agonisingly slow when
    nearly room temperature — the exponential's signature tail, and the reason forensic
    teams can estimate time of death from body temperature, inverting the same equation.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> a differential equation has one answer. Every first-order
    equation has an infinite family — one curve through each possible starting point. The
    logistic figure shows a whole sheaf of solutions as you drag P₀; the equation never
    changes. "Solving" means finding the family; "modelling" means choosing the member.
  </p>

  <h2>First-order equations II: the integrating factor</h2>
  <p>
    Separation fails when the equation is <em>linear but not separable</em>, of the form
    y′ + p(x)·y = q(x). The trick: multiply through by an <strong>integrating factor</strong>
    μ(x) = e^∫p dx — chosen so the left side becomes the derivative of the product μ·y in
    one piece (this reverses the product rule). Standard example, the charging RC circuit:
    R·dq/dt + q/C = V. Divide by R: dq/dt + q/(RC) = V/R. Here p = 1/(RC), so
    μ = e^(t/RC), and the left side collapses to the derivative of q·e^(t/RC).
    Integrating gives q·e^(t/RC) = (V/R)·RC·e^(t/RC) + C, hence
    q(t) = CV(1 − e^(−t/RC)) + C₀·e^(−t/RC). Applying q(0) = 0 kills the second term:
    <strong>q(t) = CV·(1 − e^(−t/RC))</strong>. The capacitor fills toward its ceiling CV
    exponentially, completing 63% of the journey in one <strong>time constant</strong>
    τ = RC, 95% in three, 99% in five — engineers quote "five time constants to fully
    charged" from exactly this arithmetic. The same mathematics (with different letters)
    describes drug concentration reaching steady state in a patient on a drip, and a
    thermometer warming to air temperature.
  </p>

  <h2>Second-order equations: springs, swings and sound</h2>
  <p>
    Newton's F = ma makes every mechanics problem a second-order equation. The cleanest
    case, a mass on a spring, obeys m·x″ = −k·x (Hooke's law: force proportional and
    opposite to displacement). Guess a solution: x = cos(ωt) works, because its second
    derivative is −ω²·cos(ωt) = −(k/m)·cos(ωt) provided <strong>ω = √(k/m)</strong>. The
    general solution x = A·cos(ωt) + B·sin(ωt) carries two constants — second order means
    two initial conditions (where, and how fast). The predicted frequency is testable: a
    1 kg mass on a 100 N/m spring oscillates at ω = √100 = 10 rad/s, i.e. about 1.6 cycles
    per second, and the experiment agrees.
  </p>
  <p>
    Add friction (a force −c·x′) and the solutions split into three regimes: <strong>underdamped</strong>
    (bouncy — sine waves inside a decaying exponential envelope, the behaviour of a car's
    suspension and of a struck guitar string), <strong>overdamped</strong> (sluggish — the
    door closer that eases shut without oscillating), and the knife-edge <strong>critically
    damped</strong> case, the fastest possible return to rest without overshoot, which is
    what your car's shock absorbers are tuned for. Swap friction for an external push
    F₀·cos(Ωt) and you meet <strong>resonance</strong>: when the driving frequency Ω
    matches the natural frequency ω, the amplitude grows without bound in the ideal
    equation — the reason soldiers break step on bridges, an opera singer can shatter a
    glass, and MRI machines push protons at exactly their Larmor frequency. Real systems
    cap the growth through damping, but the peak response at resonance can still be
    enormous.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> Newton's second law pushed us to second order, where two initial
    conditions select among sine-and-cosine families, and damping or driving splits the
    behaviour into named regimes. Next: reading where a system ends up without solving
    anything at all.
  </p>

  <h2>Equilibrium and stability: reading equations without solving them</h2>
  <p>
    Often the question is not "what is the exact curve?" but "where does it end up?"
    An <strong>equilibrium</strong> is a value where the rate is zero — the population stops
    changing. For the logistic law the equilibria are P = 0 and P = pₘₐₓ, and their
    character differs: nudge P slightly above 0 and growth pushes it back up <em>away</em>
    from 0 (unstable), while any deviation from pₘₐₓ is pulled back (stable). You can read
    this off the sign of dP/dt on a <strong>phase line</strong> without solving anything:
    between 0 and pₘₐₓ the rate is positive, above pₘₐₓ negative — arrows point toward
    pₘₐₓ from both sides. This qualitative toolkit extends to systems (predator–prey
    models orbit their equilibrium rather than settling on it) and is the working method of
    the nonlinear-dynamics research literature, where most equations cannot be solved
    exactly but their equilibria can still be classified completely.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "an equilibrium is a resting point, so the system always
  settles there eventually." Equilibrium means only that the rate is zero <em>at</em> that value
  — it says nothing about whether the system will stay. Stability is a separate question, read
  off the phase line: for the logistic law, any nudge above P = 0 grows away (unstable), while
  every deviation from pₘₐₓ is pulled back (stable). Two equilibria, one equation, opposite
  characters — the arrows on the phase line, not the equilibria's location, carry that
  information.</p>

  <h2>When symbols fail: Euler's method</h2>
  <p>
    Most differential equations — the three-body problem, real weather, the Navier–Stokes
    equations of the fluid-dynamics lesson — have no formula solution. The fallback is
    disarmingly simple. <strong>Euler's method</strong>: from the current point, step
    forward a small h using the slope the equation prescribes. For y′ = y with y(0) = 1 and
    h = 0.2: y₁ = 1 + 0.2×1 = 1.2; y₂ = 1.2 + 0.2×1.2 = 1.44; y₃ = 1.44 + 0.2×1.44 = 1.728.
    The true value at t = 0.6 is e^0.6 ≈ 1.822 — Euler undershoots because each step uses
    the slope at the <em>start</em> of the step while the true slope keeps rising. Halving
    h roughly halves the error (first-order accuracy); professional codes use
    Runge–Kutta schemes that sample the slope several times per step for fourth-order
    accuracy, and adapt h automatically — tiny steps through fast-changing regions, long
    strides through calm ones. Your phone's weather forecast is one enormous system of
    differential equations solved by exactly such a scheme, on a three-dimensional grid,
    every six hours.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> a numerical answer means the model was "solved", so any
    deviation from reality is arithmetic error. Usually the gap is <strong>model error</strong>
    (the equation omits something — friction, delays, stochasticity), not integration error.
    Distinguishing the two — refining the solver versus refining the equation — is the
    central judgement call of computational science.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Recall: what is the difference between a general solution and a particular solution, and what role does the initial condition play?</li>
    <li>Application: coffee sits at 90 °C in a 20 °C room, with cooling constant k = 0.07 per minute. How long until the excess temperature above the room falls to a quarter of its starting value?</li>
    <li>Why does Euler's method systematically undershoot the true solution of y′ = y, and what does that reveal about the method's structure?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) The general solution is the whole family of functions that
    satisfies the equation, with undetermined constants; the particular solution is the one
    member an initial condition selects — the law of change describes a family of futures, and
    the present state chooses among them. (2) The excess decays exponentially: 70 times
    e^(−0.07t) equals 17.5 exactly when e^(−0.07t) = 1/4, so t = ln 4 over 0.07, about 19.8
    minutes — two half-lives of the excess. (3) Each Euler step
    uses the slope at the <em>start</em> of the step, but for y′ = y the true slope keeps rising
    across the step, so every step falls short and the shortfall compounds. Undershooting is
    baked into sampling the slope once per step, which is why
    Runge–Kutta schemes sample it several times.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A differential equation is a rule for the slope at every point; solving means finding the graph that obeys the rule everywhere.</li>
    <li>Order is the highest derivative appearing; the general solution is a family, and initial conditions select the particular member.</li>
    <li>Separation solves y-stuff times dy/dx equals x-stuff, and yields the exponential: growth and decay are one equation with opposite signs.</li>
    <li>Linear, non-separable equations yield to the integrating factor; RC charging fills 63% of the way in one time constant.</li>
    <li>Second-order equations carry two constants; damping splits solutions into under-, over- and critically damped regimes; resonance is drive frequency meeting natural frequency.</li>
    <li>Equilibria classify as stable or unstable from the phase line's arrows, without solving anything.</li>
    <li>Euler's method steps along the prescribed slope; model error is a different beast from integration error.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The advanced lesson <em>Integration</em> develops the other half of the toolkit this page
    kept borrowing — integrating both sides, reversing the product rule. The advanced lesson
    <em>Logic &amp; Proof Techniques</em> houses the existence-and-uniqueness questions raised
    below: Picard's theorem is a proof, not a computation.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Existence and uniqueness.</strong> When may we assume a solution exists at all,
    let alone a unique one? Picard's theorem answers: if the right-hand side is continuous
    and reasonably smooth (Lipschitz) in y, a unique solution passes through every initial
    point. Where the condition fails, uniqueness can fail too: y′ = √y with y(0) = 0
    admits both the solution y = 0 and y = t²/4 — the mathematics of a match that either
    ignites immediately or never, sitting at an unstable equilibrium. That theorem is the
    licence behind every simulation in this atlas: without it, "run the model forward"
    would not even be well-defined.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Linearity and superposition.</strong> For linear equations, solutions can be
    added: if y₁ and y₂ solve the homogeneous equation y″ + ω²y = 0, so does any
    combination A·y₁ + B·y₂ — the reason a guitar string's sound is a chord of pure modes
    and the Fourier methods of signal processing work at all. Nonlinear equations surrender
    this luxury: for the logistic law, two valid solutions do not average to a valid
    solution. Nearly all of the interesting mathematics of the last century — solitons,
    shock waves, chaos — lives in that nonlinearity.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Chaos: deterministic yet unpredictable.</strong> Some low-dimensional systems —
    the Lorenz equations distilled from convection, the forced pendulum, the three-body
    problem — exhibit <strong>sensitive dependence on initial conditions</strong>: two
    starts differing by one part in a million diverge to completely different futures
    within a few characteristic times, the difference growing like e^(λt) with a positive
    Lyapunov exponent λ. This is not randomness (the equations are fully deterministic) and
    not numerical error (better arithmetic does not help); it is a structural property that
    caps forecast horizons. It is why weather is predictable for roughly two weeks and
    climate for a century — the climate question asks for statistics of the attractor,
    not the state of a specific day, a distinction the climate-modelling lesson builds on.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open problems.</strong> The Clay Mathematics Institute will pay one million
    dollars for progress on the Navier–Stokes existence problem: nobody can prove that the
    equations governing every fluid on Earth always have smooth solutions in three
    dimensions, or exhibit a initial condition that develops a singularity (a place where
    the maths itself blows up). Meanwhile the applied frontier is learning-based: neural
    networks trained to approximate solution operators ("physics-informed machine learning")
    now give fast approximate solvers for equations too big for classical methods — with
    the open question of when they can be trusted. The equations nature speaks are old;
    the ways of listening are still being invented.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Boyce &amp; DiPrima, <em>Elementary Differential Equations</em>; Strogatz, <em>Nonlinear Dynamics and Chaos</em>; OpenStax, <em>Calculus Volume 2</em> (ch. 8, Introduction to Differential Equations).</p>
</TopicPage>
